# Control structures

## Flags

In ANS, **true** is `-1`, **false** is `0`. Tests use `if` / `else` / `then` with a single flag on stack.

```forth
: positive? ( n -- flag )
  0> ;
```

Do not assume `1` is true unless your project standard says so.

## if / else / then

```forth
: abs ( n -- |n| )
  dup 0< if negate then ;
```

Each branch must leave the **same** stack effect unless documented otherwise.

## begin / until / again / while / repeat

```forth
: wait-key ( -- key )
  begin key? until key ;
```

Use `leave` inside `do … loop` only when early exit is required; document stack effect of `leave` for your system.

## counted loops

```forth
: fill ( c addr u -- )
  0 ?do dup i + c! loop drop ;
```

Index is `i` (and `j` in nested loops). Bounds: `( limit start -- )` for `?do` / `+loop` in ANS.

## return stack in loops

`>r` / `r>` for temporary storage in complex control flow — always document `(R …)` and ensure `r>` runs on all exit paths (no return stack leaks).

**Do not mix `>r`/`r@` with `DO` / `?DO`.** `DO` / `?DO` push the loop counter and limit on the return stack; `R@` inside the loop body returns the loop index `I`, not the value you pushed before the loop. If a counted loop needs an extra parameter:

- restructure to keep it on the data stack (often with one extra `OVER` / `ROT`);
- pass it via a `VALUE` written outside the loop and `TO`-updated;
- on **Gforth**, bind it with `{ name }` locals (see `forth-dialect-gforth.mdc`);
- or replace `DO` with `BEGIN … WHILE … REPEAT` and manage the counter yourself.

## case / of / endof (if available)

Use when dispatching on multiple discrete values; keep each `of` branch stack-balanced.

## State smart words

Words like `if` are immediate and compile branches. Do not manually `compile,` control words unless building defining words.
